======================================================================================================================
1.  STRUCTURE: the disformal term and the scalar stress share the tensor d_i phi d_j phi (sympy)
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] A1 (STRUCTURE) the traceless part of the disformal metric correction is PROPORTIONAL to the traceless part of the F(X) scalar's anisotropic stress, with a constant ratio B/F_X independent of the field direction.  So a disformal B can cancel the slip POINTWISE, not just at one radius -- the escape is real at the level of tensor structure   (traceless(dg~_ij) / traceless(T_ij^scalar) = B/F_X  for every component; the isotropic F g_ij piece of the stress does not slip and does not need cancelling)

======================================================================================================================
2.  THE IDENTITY: no-slip fixes B phi'^2, and the same B phi'^2 is the light-cone tilt
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] A2 (THE IDENTITY) the no-slip condition fixes B phi'^2 = 2(Psi - Phi) pointwise, and substituting that SAME value into the ratio of the GW null cone to the light null cone gives (c_GW - c_light)/c = (Psi - Phi): the slip cancelled equals the speed difference created, to leading order, with nothing left to tune   (no-slip: B phi'^2 = -2*Phi + 2*Psi;  tilt = Bphi2/2 = (Psi - Phi))

======================================================================================================================
3.  THE NUMBER: how big is the MOND slip, hence the tilt, both footings
======================================================================================================================
  Case 2 says the slip is O(1) x Phi in the MOND regime.  Parametrize (Psi - Phi) = eps * Phi_MOND with eps in
  [0.1, 1].  Phi_MOND is the potential where a galaxy is deep-MOND, v_flat^2 / c^2 with v_flat^4 = G M a_0 (BTFR).
     NGC 4993 host (~1e11 Msun)   Phi_MOND = 3.922e-07  ->  tilt = eps x 3.922e-07
     Milky Way (~6e10 Msun)       Phi_MOND = 3.038e-07  ->  tilt = eps x 3.038e-07
  [PASS] A3 (THE MAGNITUDE) in the MOND outskirts of an ordinary galaxy the slip-cancelling tilt is of order 1e-7, eight orders of magnitude above the GW170817 bound, on both footings and for any eps down to 0.01   (canonical 3.92e-07, alt 4.31e-07 per unit eps; even eps = 0.01 gives 3.9e-09 against a bound of 1e-15 -- a factor 4e+06)

======================================================================================================================
4.  GW170817's ACTUAL PATH: two galaxies' MOND outskirts plus 40 Mpc of intergalactic medium
======================================================================================================================
  The signal left NGC 4993 and arrived at Earth, so it crossed the MOND regions of BOTH galaxies and the IGM.
  Path-averaged tilt = (1/D) Integral (Psi - Phi) dl.  Model: each galaxy contributes a segment L_gal of deep-MOND
  outskirts at tilt eps*Phi_MOND; the IGM contributes the whole path at a tilt suppressed by (g_IGM/g_gal)^2, since
  B phi'^2 goes as the square of the scalar gradient and in deep MOND that gradient carries the acceleration.
     eps =  1.0:  galactic segments 1.74e-09   IGM alone 3.48e-13   bound 1e-15   -> over by 2e+06 (gal) / 3e+02 (IGM)
     eps =  0.1:  galactic segments 1.74e-10   IGM alone 3.48e-14   bound 1e-15   -> over by 2e+05 (gal) / 3e+01 (IGM)
  [PASS] A4 (THE KILL) the two galaxies' MOND outskirts alone put the path-averaged GW/light speed difference five to six orders of magnitude over the GW170817 bound, for eps anywhere from 0.1 to 1, on both footings   (eps=1: 2e+06x over; eps=0.1: 2e+05x over (canonical); alt footing eps=0.1: 2e+05x)
  [PASS] A5 (EVEN THE IGM ALONE) suppressing the tilt by the square of the acceleration ratio, the 40 Mpc of intergalactic medium by itself still exceeds the bound -- so the kill does not depend on the galactic path model   (IGM alone, eps=0.1: 35x over (canonical), 38x (alt); eps=1: 348x)

======================================================================================================================
5.  AGAINST INTEREST: Cassini is NOT the killer -- this trades gate 3 for gate 6 and nothing else
======================================================================================================================
  In the solar system the acceleration is >> a_0, mu -> 1, and the scalar carries essentially no force: phi' -> 0.
  The disformal spatial correction B phi'^2 therefore vanishes there, and with A = 1 the matter-frame g~_00 = g_00,
  so gamma_PPN is untouched.  Quantify with the framework's own kernel: the scalar's share of the force is 1 - 1/nu.
     scalar force share at Saturn: canonical 0.00e+00, alt 0.00e+00  (so phi'^2 is down by its square)
  [PASS] A6 (against interest, and it sharpens the verdict) the disformal correction is negligible at Cassini because the scalar carries no force at solar-system accelerations, so the escape does NOT fail on gamma_PPN.  It fails on ONE gate only, c_T, and it fails there by construction: the mechanism that fixes lensing is the mechanism that tilts the light cone   (disformal term at Saturn ~ (scalar share)^2 x Phi ~ 0.0e+00, far below the Cassini |gamma-1| bound 2.3e-5)

======================================================================================================================
6.  mutation controls and calibration against the repo's own Case 3a
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: with B = 0 the light cone coincides with the GW cone (tilt exactly zero) and the slip is back -- the tilt is caused by the cancellation, not by the machinery   (tilt(B=0) = 0; slip(B=0) = Psi - Phi)
  [PASS] M2 mutation: with no MOND force (scalar gradient zero) both the slip and the required B phi'^2 vanish, recovering GR with Phi = Psi and c_GW = c_light   (traceless scalar stress = 0 at phi' = 0, so nothing to cancel and nothing to tilt)
  [PASS] M3 calibration: the tilt found here (~1e-7 in MOND zones) is the same order as Case 3a's lambda = -2e-7, which the repo killed at 1e5x (delay) to 1e7-1e9x (c_T); the factors here fall in that range, so the two kills are mutually consistent   (this file: 2e+06x over GW170817; Case 3a: 1e5x-1e9x)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  Door A's frame-free branch had ONE named escape from the slip kill: a disformal matter coupling.  It is real at
  the level of structure -- the disformal correction shares the tensor form of the scalar's anisotropic stress, so
  it cancels the slip pointwise -- and it dies on one line of algebra: the no-slip condition fixes B phi'^2 =
  2(Psi - Phi), and that same quantity IS the fractional GW/light speed difference.  The slip you cancel is the
  tilt you create.  In galaxy outskirts the slip is ~1e-7; GW170817 allows 1e-15.  Along that event's actual path
  the excess is 1e5-1e6x from the two galaxies' MOND regions, and the IGM alone still exceeds the bound.  Cassini
  is untouched, so this is a clean trade of gate 3 for gate 6, with no parameter in between.
  DOOR A, FRAME-FREE BRANCH: CLOSED, conditional on A1 (the coupling is disformal of the Bekenstein form) and on
  Case 2's slip being O(Phi) in the MOND regime.  Together with the committed framed-branch kill (alpha_1) this
  leaves door A one leg: prove the alpha_1 kill general beyond the aether class, or exhibit a framed coupling that
  makes MOND without vector-scalar mixing.  That is the next computation, and it is the last one door A has.

